# What a fuzz run produces, and what it must not commit.
#
# `seeds/` IS tracked — it is the reviewable input, derived from real repo files
# by `src/bin/seed.rs` and regenerable with `scripts/fuzz.sh seed`. Everything
# below is the fuzzer's own working state:
#
#   corpus/    — the growing, machine-found corpus. Thousands of small files
#                that nobody reads, that change on every run, and that would
#                make `scripts/lint-repo-hygiene.sh`'s untracked-file sweep walk
#                a directory the size of the build. `scripts/fuzz.sh` copies
#                `seeds/<target>/` into it before every run, so a fresh clone
#                loses nothing but machine time.
#   artifacts/ — a crash, a leak or a timeout, written as the exact input that
#                produced it. Deliberately ignored: a reproducer belongs in the
#                owning crate's test file as a regression test, minimised with
#                `cargo fuzz tmin`, not as an opaque blob here.
#   coverage/  — `cargo fuzz coverage` output.
#   target/    — a second target directory; the sanitizer build has its own
#                fingerprint and shares nothing with the root one.
corpus/
artifacts/
coverage/
target/
